package custom;

import robocode.*;
import java.awt.geom.Point2D;
import java.awt.Color;

/**
 * MyTank - improved AdvancedRobot bot.
 *
 * Strategy:
 *  - Radar: lock on to the last scanned enemy and keep sweeping to reacquire quickly.
 *  - Gun: linear-prediction targeting (assume enemy continues at current velocity/heading),
 *         with power scaled by distance (more power up close, less far away to save energy).
 *  - Movement: perpendicular "orbit" strafing around the enemy at a preferred distance,
 *         with periodic randomized direction reversals to avoid simple targeting bots,
 *         wall-avoidance (clamp target waypoint to a safe inset rectangle so we never
 *         plan a move that drives us into a wall), plus an explicit stuck-watchdog that
 *         forces a strong escape maneuver if our velocity has been ~0 for too long.
 *  - Defensive: on being hit, reverse/change strafing direction.
 *
 * IMPORTANT BUGFIX (see README_agent.md "wall-stuck bug" writeup): analysis of
 * round-0 match logs showed that in EVERY losing game, our tank got physically
 * wedged against a wall (x/y frozen for 700+ consecutive turns) because the old
 * onScannedRobot() movement logic had NO wall-avoidance at all -- it would happily
 * keep commanding "ahead" straight into a wall every single scan (which happens
 * nearly every turn once the enemy is visible), fighting against onHitWall()'s
 * one-shot setBack(80) recovery and effectively canceling out into a near-zero-net
 * -movement standoff. While frozen like this, both robots' energy drains steadily
 * every turn (looks like the environment's anti-stalemate/inactivity decay), and
 * whichever robot had less energy banked before the freeze started loses that race.
 * This rewrite fixes it at the source: every planned move is clamped to a safe
 * inset rectangle of the battlefield *before* being issued, and a watchdog detects
 * near-zero velocity over several consecutive scans and forces a hard escape burn
 * toward the field center, breaking any wall standoff quickly instead of letting it
 * drag on for hundreds of turns.
 *
 * This bot only relies on our own AdvancedRobot API usage; it doesn't assume anything
 * about the opponent's implementation.
 */
public class MyTank extends AdvancedRobot {

    // Preferred distance to keep from the enemy while orbiting. Round 8: reduced
    // from 300 -> 220 so we spend more time inside the 150-350 bulletPowerForDistance
    // band (2.9 power) rather than mostly straddling right at the edge of it, since
    // this rung's opponent (it_economics__ite_bomax) is nearly stationary and very
    // inaccurate (4%) -- closing distance a bit costs little in dodge risk but lets
    // us land harder shots more often. Still leaves ~150px of margin from the 150
    // threshold for the 3.0-power band, and plenty of room from walls/ram range.
    private static final double PREFERRED_DISTANCE = 220;

    // Keep planned waypoints at least this far from any wall.
    // Round 31: increased 70 -> 95. Analysis of this rung's losses (tibola__markiv,
    // see README_agent.md round 31 notes) showed avg 3.3 walls/game (on the higher
    // end of this file's history) and 6+ HIT_WALL events in at least one lost game,
    // each costing ~3 energy for nothing -- pure unforced waste. A bigger safety
    // margin gives the turn-then-move commands more room to actually complete
    // their turn before the translation carries us past the boundary, at the cost
    // of slightly less usable battlefield area (minor, 800x600 field still leaves
    // plenty of room).
    private static final double WALL_MARGIN = 95;

    // Strafing direction: 1 or -1. Flips periodically / when hit.
    private int moveDirection = 1;

    // Turn counter used to occasionally flip strafing direction to be less predictable.
    private int strafeTimer = 0;

    // Last known enemy info, used for linear targeting and for continuing to track
    // even in ticks where we don't get a fresh scan.
    private double enemyDistance = Double.MAX_VALUE;

    // Previous scan's enemy heading/time, used to estimate the enemy's turn rate
    // (radians/tick) so gun prediction can account for a curving/orbiting enemy,
    // not just one that flies in a straight line. Reset whenever we haven't seen
    // the enemy in a while (radar re-acquire after loss of lock), since a stale
    // turn-rate estimate across a scan gap could be wildly wrong.
    private double prevEnemyHeading = 0;
    private long prevEnemyScanTime = -1;
    private double enemyHeadingRate = 0;

    // Turn number when we last saw an enemy; used to trigger fallback "search"
    // movement if we haven't scanned anyone for a while (e.g. radar temporarily
    // lost lock, or enemy is outside our current sweep). Prevents us from being
    // a stationary sitting duck if onScannedRobot stops firing for any reason.
    private long lastScanTime = -1000;
    private int searchTurnDir = 1;

    // Stuck-watchdog: counts consecutive onScannedRobot calls where our velocity
    // has stayed near zero despite us commanding movement. If this gets too high,
    // we're probably wedged against a wall (or another robot) and need to force a
    // strong escape maneuver rather than keep doing the same thing.
    private int stuckScanCount = 0;

    // Round 14 bugfix: detect "stuck ramming" mutual-collision lock. Log analysis
    // (see README_agent.md round 14 notes) found real games where onHitRobot()'s
    // round-12 "press forward into the enemy" logic gets stuck in a persistent
    // loop -- HitRobotEvent kept firing every single tick for 100+ consecutive
    // ticks while our x/y AND radar/gun heading stayed byte-identical the whole
    // time (velocity pinned at 0.0), bleeding 0.6 energy/tick (pure collision
    // damage, not inactivity decay) with zero compensating benefit. Root cause
    // looks like: if the enemy is itself wedged against a wall (its own bug) and
    // we keep charging straight at it every time onHitRobot() fires, we can end
    // up in a mutual standoff where neither robot's commanded movement can
    // actually make progress, but the collision keeps re-triggering every turn
    // forever -- onHitRobot() had no awareness at all of "have I actually made
    // progress since the last collision", unlike the analogous stuck-watchdog
    // that onScannedRobot() already had (added in round 3) for wall standoffs.
    // These fields let onHitRobot() detect that pattern and break off instead of
    // blindly re-charging forever.
    private double lastHitRobotX = Double.NaN;
    private double lastHitRobotY = Double.NaN;
    private int hitRobotStationaryCount = 0;

    // Cooldown after a forced ramming disengage, during which onScannedRobot()'s
    // opportunistic-ramming trigger (enemyDistance < 60) is suppressed, so we
    // don't immediately re-charge back into the same stuck situation the instant
    // we back off from it.
    private long rammingCooldownUntil = -1;

    // Round 20 bugfix: unified "escape mode" to stop onScannedRobot() and
    // onHitRobot() from fighting each other over movement commands.
    //
    // Root cause found this round (see README_agent.md round 20 notes): Robocode
    // dispatches events within a turn in DESCENDING priority order
    // (HitRobotEvent default priority 40, ScannedRobotEvent default priority 10 --
    // verified via `javap -p -c -constants` on the compiled event classes), so
    // whenever both fire in the same turn (extremely common while pinned against
    // an enemy that's also still in radar view), onHitRobot() runs FIRST and sets
    // a disengage turn+move command, then onScannedRobot() runs SECOND and
    // unconditionally overwrites it with fresh orbit/ram-trigger/stuck-watchdog
    // movement commands of its own -- silently discarding whatever onHitRobot()
    // just decided, every single tick. Real match logs (round 1 vs
    // pez__droidpoet, e.g. sim_10.jsonl t=172-276) showed our velocity pinned at
    // *exactly* 0.0 for 100+ consecutive ticks despite onHitRobot()'s round-19
    // "fixed" disengage command being reissued every tick -- it never got a
    // chance to actually take effect because onScannedRobot() kept clobbering it
    // moments later in the same turn.
    //
    // Fix: once ANY stuck-detector (either onHitRobot()'s position-based one, or
    // onScannedRobot()'s pre-existing velocity-based stuckScanCount one) decides
    // we need to escape, we enter a shared "escape mode" for a fixed number of
    // ticks (escapeUntil). While in escape mode, BOTH event handlers reissue the
    // *exact same* turn-and-ahead command (toward escapeHeadingRad, computed once
    // when escape mode began) and skip all their other movement logic entirely --
    // so no matter which handler happens to run last in a given turn, they agree
    // on what to do, and the command has a real chance to actually execute for
    // long enough to build up velocity and create real separation, instead of
    // being reset from scratch (or overridden by conflicting logic) every tick.
    private long escapeUntil = -1;
    private double escapeHeadingRad = 0;
    // Round 36: dedupe guard. Multiple event handlers (onHitWall, onHitByBullet,
    // onHitRobot, onScannedRobot) can all legitimately fire within the SAME game
    // tick, and each of them calls reissueEscape() while escape mode is active
    // (see the various "if (getTime() < escapeUntil) { reissueEscape(); return; }"
    // guards below). reissueEscape() itself does stuck-detection bookkeeping
    // (escapeStuckTicks++/reset, and rotating escapeHeadingRad by 90 degrees every
    // 3 stuck ticks) that is only meant to run ONCE PER REAL GAME TICK -- but with
    // no dedupe, calling it 2+ times in the same tick (very common: a wall-stuck
    // tank with an enemy still in radar view fires both onHitWall() AND
    // onScannedRobot() the same tick) silently doubles the stuck-tick count rate,
    // causing the 90-degree rotation to trigger roughly 2x too fast. Real-match
    // logs (round 36, see README_agent.md) showed this turning the turn-based
    // wall-escape (round 35) into a permanent oscillation: body heading bouncing back
    // and forth every 1-2 ticks, NEVER converging on any single target heading
    // long enough to actually build velocity and move away from the wall --
    // explaining 1000+-tick full-game wall-stuck freezes. Fix: only actually do
    // the stuck-detection/movement-issuing work once per unique getTime() value;
    // redundant same-tick calls from a second/third handler are now harmless no-ops.
    private long lastEscapeReissueTick = -1;
    // Round 23: the round-20 escape mode fixed the event-priority override problem
    // (both handlers agreeing on the same command), but real logs still showed
    // 100+ tick freezes with velocity pinned at exactly 0.0 the whole time (see
    // README_agent.md round 23 notes, sim_131.jsonl vs it_economics__ite_claptrap).
    // Root cause: the chosen escape heading can itself be blocked (e.g. the
    // enemy robot is sitting directly in the only direction that's away from the
    // corner/wall we're pinned against), so reissuing the SAME blocked heading
    // forever never makes progress -- we only ever escaped once the enemy
    // happened to die and stop blocking us. Fix: track whether we're actually
    // moving while in escape mode; if not, rotate the escape heading by 90
    // degrees every few stuck ticks to search for a direction that's actually
    // clear, instead of blindly trusting the first computed heading forever.
    private double escapeBaseHeadingRad = 0;
    private int escapeRotationSteps = 0;
    private int escapeStuckTicks = 0;
    private double lastEscapeX = Double.NaN;
    private double lastEscapeY = Double.NaN;

    // Round 25: "no-turn" ram-escape mode. Decompiled javadoc for HitRobotEvent
    // (javadoc/robocode/HitRobotEvent.html) confirms a mechanism nobody had
    // checked in 24 rounds of chasing this bug: "If isMyFault() returns true
    // then your robot's movement (including turning) will have stopped and
    // been marked complete." I.e. while we are actively moving/turning TOWARD
    // a robot we're touching, the game engine cancels BOTH our translation
    // AND our rotation for that tick -- not just translation. Real match
    // traces (round 25 notes, sim_7.jsonl vs it_economics__ite_ctbot) showed
    // body heading (getHeadingRadians()) frozen byte-identical for 60+
    // consecutive ticks during a stuck-ramming lock, which the turn-based
    // escape mode (rounds 20/23) could never fix: rotating toward a computed
    // "away" heading requires several ticks of partial turning, and during
    // every one of those ticks our heading is *still* pointed enough toward
    // the enemy that "ahead" motion counts as "moving toward" it, so the
    // turn itself keeps getting cancelled before it can ever complete --
    // a total deadlock. Fix: when escaping specifically from a robot-contact
    // lock, issue ZERO turn and only setBack()/setAhead() along our EXISTING
    // heading (whichever one actually points away from the enemy already) --
    // this can never be blocked by the isMyFault mechanism since it requires
    // no rotation to take effect, sidestepping the trap entirely.
    private boolean escapeNoTurn = false;
    private boolean escapeMoveBack = false;


    // Round 16 addition: "dodge on fire" reactive evasion. Detect the enemy
    // just firing a bullet (their energy dropped by an amount consistent with
    // a bullet's power cost, Rules.html says firing costs exactly bulletPower
    // energy up front regardless of hit/miss) and react immediately by
    // flipping/randomizing our strafe direction and resetting the strafe
    // timer, so whatever aim the enemy used (linear/circular prediction based
    // on our position and heading *at the moment they fired*) is more likely
    // to be wrong by the time their bullet arrives. This is a well-known
    // simple defensive tactic distinct from full wave-surfing (which would
    // require tracking bullet origin/velocity precisely to compute a safe
    // lateral offset) -- much lower risk to add without local battle-testing
    // available, since it only adds an extra, occasional direction flip to
    // the existing orbit-strafing movement rather than replacing it.
    private double prevEnemyEnergy = -1;

    // Round 20: shared escape-mode helper. See the field comments above for why
    // this exists. Always turns toward the given absolute field heading and moves
    // forward -- callers should compute headingRad as an escape direction that's
    // already away from whatever we're stuck against (enemy and/or wall).
    private void beginEscape(double headingRad, int durationTicks) {
        escapeNoTurn = false;
        escapeBaseHeadingRad = headingRad;
        escapeHeadingRad = headingRad;
        escapeRotationSteps = 0;
        escapeStuckTicks = 0;
        lastEscapeX = getX();
        lastEscapeY = getY();
        escapeUntil = getTime() + durationTicks;
        reissueEscape();
    }

    // Round 25: no-turn variant, for breaking a robot-contact ("stuck ramming")
    // lock specifically -- see the escapeNoTurn field comment above for why
    // this exists and why the turn-based beginEscape() above cannot reliably
    // work for this particular failure mode. moveBack=true means "the enemy is
    // roughly ahead of us, so back away along our current heading"; false
    // means "the enemy is roughly behind us, so continue ahead along our
    // current heading" -- either way, zero turn is requested, only straight-
    // line translation along whatever heading we already have.
    private void beginNoTurnEscape(boolean moveBack, int durationTicks) {
        escapeNoTurn = true;
        escapeMoveBack = moveBack;
        escapeRotationSteps = 0;
        escapeStuckTicks = 0;
        lastEscapeX = getX();
        lastEscapeY = getY();
        escapeUntil = getTime() + durationTicks;
        reissueEscape();
    }

    // Round 40 fix (see README_agent.md round 40 notes): reissueEscape() and
    // several other event-handler code paths (onHitWall, onHitRobot,
    // onHitByBullet, and onScannedRobot's own ramming-trigger/orbit-movement
    // tail) used to call execute() manually after queuing their turn/move
    // commands. Manually calling execute() from INSIDE an event handler is a
    // known Robocode pitfall: HitWallEvent (priority 30) and HitRobotEvent
    // (priority 40) are dispatched BEFORE ScannedRobotEvent (priority 10)
    // within the same game turn, and calling execute() from the higher-
    // priority handler can cause the engine to consider that turn "done"
    // before the still-pending, lower-priority onScannedRobot() dispatch for
    // the SAME turn ever runs -- silently eating that turn's scan event.
    // Traced this directly in a real losing game (sim_26.jsonl, round 40):
    // once onHitWall() first fired (and called execute()), our gun heading
    // (gh) froze byte-identical for 30+ consecutive ticks even though our
    // own radar kept sweeping fine (radar is driven independently from
    // run()'s own loop) -- i.e. onScannedRobot() had stopped being invoked
    // at all, starving our gun of new aim updates and our fire logic of any
    // chance to run, while the opponent (not colliding, so no competing
    // high-priority handler stealing its turns) kept landing free full-power
    // hits on us the whole time. Fix: removed every manual execute() call
    // from event-handler code (onHitWall, onHitRobot, onHitByBullet,
    // onScannedRobot's tail, and this function) and rely ENTIRELY on the
    // single execute() call at the bottom of run()'s main while-loop to
    // flush queued commands once per turn, after all of that turn's event
    // handlers (regardless of priority) have had a chance to run and queue
    // their own commands -- this is the standard, documented-safe
    // AdvancedRobot pattern and avoids the priority-dependent turn-skipping
    // risk entirely.
    // Reissue the current escape command (same target heading, recomputed relative
    // turn since our own heading may have changed slightly since escape mode
    // began). Called every tick from both onScannedRobot() and onHitRobot() while
    // escapeUntil hasn't passed yet, so both handlers always agree.
    private void reissueEscape() {
        // Round 36 dedupe guard -- see lastEscapeReissueTick field comment above.
        if (getTime() == lastEscapeReissueTick) {
            return;
        }
        lastEscapeReissueTick = getTime();
        // Round 37 fix (see README_agent.md round 37 notes): round 36's dedupe
        // fix stopped the stuck-tick counter from incrementing 2x per real tick,
        // but real-match logs STILL showed permanent wall-stuck freezes (this
        // environment's own round-1/round-2 data: 32-34% win rate, avg speed
        // ~2.2, games ballooning to 900+ turns) with body heading (bh) visibly
        // oscillating back and forth every tick by exactly the max per-tick turn
        // rate (~0.1745 rad/tick at v=0), NEVER converging on escapeHeadingRad.
        // Root cause: the OLD position-only stuck check below fires "no real
        // progress" (position unchanged) even while the robot is legitimately
        // mid-turn toward a perfectly good target heading -- of course position
        // hasn't moved yet, it hasn't finished turning away from the wall to be
        // ABLE to move without immediately re-colliding! With the old 3-tick
        // threshold, escapeRotationSteps++ (rotating the target by 90 degrees,
        // i.e. ~1.571 rad) fires roughly every 3 ticks, but completing a 90-
        // degree turn from a standstill takes ~9 ticks at the ~0.1745 rad/tick
        // max turn rate -- so the target heading always moves the goalposts
        // long before the robot could ever physically reach the CURRENT one,
        // guaranteeing perpetual thrashing and zero real progress, forever.
        // Fix: only count a tick as "stuck" (for rotation-triggering purposes)
        // if the robot is ALREADY ROUGHLY ALIGNED with the current target
        // heading (i.e. genuinely done turning, not mid-turn) and STILL hasn't
        // moved -- that's the only situation that actually indicates "this
        // heading is physically blocked, try a different one". If we're still
        // mid-turn, don't touch the stuck counter at all; let the turn finish.
        double curX = getX();
        double curY = getY();
        double headingErrForStuckCheck = escapeNoTurn ? 0.0
                : robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                        escapeHeadingRad - getHeadingRadians());
        boolean roughlyAligned = escapeNoTurn || Math.abs(headingErrForStuckCheck) < 0.25;
        boolean noPositionProgress = !Double.isNaN(lastEscapeX)
                && Math.hypot(curX - lastEscapeX, curY - lastEscapeY) < 2;
        if (roughlyAligned && noPositionProgress) {
            escapeStuckTicks++;
        } else if (!roughlyAligned) {
            // Still actively turning toward the target -- not stuck, just not
            // finished yet. Don't reset all the way to 0 on every single such
            // tick either (that would let a robot that's ALMOST aligned but
            // wobbling by noise keep resetting forever); a mild decay is enough.
            if (escapeStuckTicks > 0) {
                escapeStuckTicks--;
            }
        } else {
            escapeStuckTicks = 0;
        }
        lastEscapeX = curX;
        lastEscapeY = curY;

        // Round 25: no-turn mode (see escapeNoTurn field comment) -- explicitly
        // issue a ZERO turn (never risk the isMyFault-blocks-turning trap) and
        // only translate along our existing heading. If we're still not making
        // progress after a few ticks, flip direction (maybe it's a wall behind
        // us in that direction, not the enemy) rather than trying to rotate,
        // which is exactly what we're trying to avoid here.
        if (escapeNoTurn) {
            if (escapeStuckTicks >= 3) {
                escapeMoveBack = !escapeMoveBack;
                escapeStuckTicks = 0;
            }
            setTurnRightRadians(0);
            if (escapeMoveBack) {
                setBack(100);
            } else {
                setAhead(100);
            }
            return;
        }

        if (escapeStuckTicks >= 3) {
            escapeRotationSteps++;
            escapeHeadingRad = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                    escapeBaseHeadingRad + escapeRotationSteps * (Math.PI / 2.0));
            escapeStuckTicks = 0;
        }
        double turn = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                escapeHeadingRad - getHeadingRadians());
        setTurnRightRadians(turn);
        setAhead(100);
    }

    public void run() {
        setAdjustGunForRobotTurn(true);
        setAdjustRadarForGunTurn(true);
        setColors(Color.RED, Color.BLACK, Color.YELLOW);

        // Kick off radar spinning; onScannedRobot will keep re-aiming it at the enemy.
        setTurnRadarRight(Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY);

        while (true) {
            // Round 45 addition ("wall velocity" fix): analysis of this rung's 8
            // losses (andrekorol__myfirstkiller, see README_agent.md round 45 notes)
            // showed our tank driving at FULL SPEED (v=8) in a slow, gradual curve
            // straight into a wall corner over 30+ consecutive ticks, in games
            // where the enemy was 700+ px away the entire time (i.e. NOT the
            // ramming trigger, and NOT a case the WALL_MARGIN clamp should have
            // allowed at all in principle). Root cause: Robocode's max turn rate is
            // capped by CURRENT velocity (roughly 10 - 0.75*|v| deg/tick), so at
            // v=8 we can only turn ~4 deg/tick -- far too slow to correct even a
            // moderate heading error before covering the WALL_MARGIN=95 buffer
            // distance at 8px/tick. The orbit/movement logic recomputes a fresh
            // turn+move command every scan, but since it never reduces our
            // velocity, a heading correction that needs e.g. 60+ degrees can take
            // 15+ ticks to complete while we're still moving at full speed toward
            // the wall the whole time. Fix: proactively cap our max velocity as we
            // approach any wall, so the turn-rate cap loosens up (slower robots
            // turn faster) with enough runway to actually complete a course
            // correction before contact. This is a pure per-tick global velocity
            // cap, independent of which handler issued this tick's turn/move
            // command, so it doesn't need to be duplicated into every movement
            // branch (orbit strafe, escape mode, ramming, search fallback, etc.).
            // Round 46 retune: round 45's fix DID slash wall hits (2.1 -> 0.2
            // avg walls/game in the real match), but the threshold (160) was
            // far too large -- on an 800x600 field it made roughly 72% of the
            // battlefield area count as "near a wall" and throttled our speed
            // there, tanking avg speed (6.6 -> 5.7), accuracy (34% -> 24%) and
            // ultimately win rate (97% -> 88%, see README_agent.md round 46
            // notes) by making us slow/predictable almost everywhere, not just
            // genuinely close to a wall. Fix: shrink the affected radius way
            // down (close to WALL_MARGIN's own 95px buffer, not 160) AND use a
            // quadratic falloff (frac*frac) instead of linear, so velocity
            // stays essentially at full speed (8.0) until we're genuinely deep
            // into the buffer zone, only meaningfully slowing in the last
            // ~1/3 of the threshold distance right before contact -- keeping
            // the core "give the turn time to complete" benefit while
            // affecting a much smaller fraction of normal play.
            double distToNearestWall = Math.min(
                    Math.min(getX(), getBattleFieldWidth() - getX()),
                    Math.min(getY(), getBattleFieldHeight() - getY()));
            final double WALL_SLOW_THRESHOLD = 85;
            if (distToNearestWall < WALL_SLOW_THRESHOLD) {
                double frac = distToNearestWall / WALL_SLOW_THRESHOLD;
                double cappedVel = Math.max(3.0, 8.0 * frac * frac);
                setMaxVelocity(cappedVel);
            } else {
                setMaxVelocity(8.0);
            }

            // CRITICAL RADAR-STALL FIX (see README_agent.md "radar freeze" writeup):
            // onScannedRobot() takes over the radar every time it fires, issuing a
            // small precise "lock on" turn (setTurnRadarRightRadians(radarTurn * 1.5)).
            // If that lock-on turn ever completes with the enemy no longer inside the
            // radar's arc for the next tick (e.g. we turned our body away, the enemy
            // sped off, or the lock math returned ~0 turn), NOTHING then re-issues a
            // fresh sweep -- the one-time setTurnRadarRight(INFINITY) call before this
            // loop only ever fires once, at t=0, and gets permanently overwritten the
            // very first time onScannedRobot runs. Once the radar's pending turn
            // reaches zero with no enemy in view, it just sits frozen forever: no more
            // scans, no more firing, while both robots quietly bleed energy from the
            // engine's inactivity decay. (Confirmed via log analysis of round 1's two
            // losses: our radar heading (rh) froze permanently mid-game while our body
            // kept moving, and we simply stopped firing for the rest of a 1000+ turn
            // match, losing an attrition race we should have won outright since our
            // opponent literally never moved or fired a shot either time.)
            //
            // Fix: every turn, if the radar currently has no pending turn at all
            // (getRadarTurnRemaining() == 0 -- i.e. any previous command, whether our
            // own lock-on or a stale leftover, has fully completed), unconditionally
            // re-issue an infinite spin. onScannedRobot's precise lock-on command will
            // immediately override this again on any turn we actually see the enemy,
            // so this is a pure safety net that costs nothing while actively tracking
            // and guarantees we always resume sweeping within a single tick of ever
            // losing the target, instead of potentially freezing for the rest of the
            // match.
            if (getRadarTurnRemaining() == 0) {
                setTurnRadarRight(Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY);
            }

            // Fallback "search" behavior: if we haven't scanned an enemy in a while
            // (e.g. right at the start of the round, or if we temporarily lose lock),
            // move around instead of sitting still. This makes us harder to hit and
            // helps us find enemies faster than standing in one spot spinning radar.
            if (getTime() - lastScanTime > 15 && getDistanceRemaining() == 0 && getTurnRemaining() == 0) {
                double sx = getX();
                double sy = getY();
                double edgeMargin = 100;
                if (sx < edgeMargin || sx > getBattleFieldWidth() - edgeMargin
                        || sy < edgeMargin || sy > getBattleFieldHeight() - edgeMargin) {
                    // Near a wall while blind-searching -- head toward field center
                    // instead of risking a wall hit via the plain random-turn patrol.
                    double cx = getBattleFieldWidth() / 2.0;
                    double cy = getBattleFieldHeight() / 2.0;
                    double angleToCenter = Math.atan2(cx - sx, cy - sy);
                    setTurnRightRadians(robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                            angleToCenter - getHeadingRadians()));
                    setAhead(120);
                } else {
                    if (Math.random() < 0.15) {
                        searchTurnDir = -searchTurnDir;
                    }
                    setTurnRight(30 * searchTurnDir);
                    setAhead(120);
                }
            }
            execute();
        }
    }

    public void onScannedRobot(ScannedRobotEvent e) {
        lastScanTime = getTime();
        double absBearing = getHeadingRadians() + e.getBearingRadians();
        enemyDistance = e.getDistance();

        // --- Round 16: reactive "dodge on fire" evasion ---
        // A bullet-firing energy cost is always in (0, 3] (Rules.MIN/MAX bullet
        // power); a ram-collision cost is a fixed 0.6. Both look similar, but
        // either way an extra, well-timed direction flip right when the enemy's
        // energy visibly drops is a harmless reactive juke (worst case: one more
        // flip than strictly necessary), so we don't bother distinguishing the
        // two causes precisely -- just treat any plausible drop as "maybe they
        // just fired, juke now just in case".
        if (prevEnemyEnergy >= 0) {
            double energyDrop = prevEnemyEnergy - e.getEnergy();
            if (energyDrop >= 0.09 && energyDrop <= 3.05) {
                if (Math.random() < 0.7) {
                    moveDirection = -moveDirection;
                }
                strafeTimer = 0;
            }
        }
        prevEnemyEnergy = e.getEnergy();

        // --- Radar lock: point radar straight at enemy plus a little extra sweep so we
        // don't lose lock if it moves fast. ---
        double radarTurn = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                absBearing - getRadarHeadingRadians());
        setTurnRadarRightRadians(radarTurn * 1.5);

        // --- Gun: linear prediction targeting ---
        double bulletPower = bulletPowerForDistance(e.getDistance(), e.getVelocity());
        // Round 12 tuning: corrected energy-management math. Round 11's version of
        // this block throttled bullet power down to a cheap 1.0 whenever OUR OWN
        // energy was low, reasoning (incorrectly) that low accuracy (~17% against
        // this opponent, pez__gf1) made firing a bad bet in isolation. That
        // reasoning only looked at our own energy in a vacuum. The actual Robocode
        // economics (Rules.getBulletHitBonus == 3x bulletPower energy refunded to
        // the shooter on a hit, on top of Rules.getBulletDamage == 6*power-2 dealt
        // to the target for power>1) mean the metric that actually matters is the
        // *relative* energy swing between us and the enemy per shot, which works
        // out to:
        //     swing(P, p) = p*(9P - 2) - P   [p = hit probability, P = bullet power]
        // At our actual measured accuracy (p ~= 0.17), swing(P) is INCREASING in P
        // for every power level we use (3.0/2.9/2.2/1.5) -- i.e. higher power is
        // *better* for relative energy swing at this accuracy, not worse. Round
        // 11's throttle-to-1.0-when-low change was therefore net counter-productive
        // (it lowered our relative swing precisely when we could least afford to
        // fall further behind), and matches the observed result: round 11's real
        // match (see README_agent.md round 12 notes) showed the tie rate and score
        // were flat-to-slightly-worse after that change, not improved.
        // Kept/changed this round:
        //  1. Finishing triage (kept, this part is fine): if the enemy's remaining
        //     energy is <= 16 (a single power-3 hit could kill them outright),
        //     always use max power -- worth it for the potential kill.
        //  2. Own-energy-low throttle-down (REMOVED): no longer reduce power when
        //     our own energy is low; keep using the normal distance-based power,
        //     since the math above shows that's better for relative swing, not
        //     worse, even (especially) when we're behind.
        //  3. NEW "press the advantage" rule: if we have a big energy lead over the
        //     enemy (getEnergy() - e.getEnergy() > 15), use max power to try to
        //     close out the fight decisively rather than let a long, grindy
        //     long-range battle continue -- round-0/1 logs show a persistent ~14%
        //     tie rate from long games (700-1500+ turns) where both sides slowly
        //     bleed to 0 together; pressing a clear advantage harder should convert
        //     more of those into decisive wins before mutual attrition/inactivity
        //     decay has a chance to catch up to us too.
        // Round 18 fix: the finishing/press-advantage overrides below used to
        // force bulletPower to a flat 3.0 unconditionally. Against a fast/erratic
        // mover (see round 17's velocity-based cap in bulletPowerForDistance()
        // above, and README_agent.md's round 18 notes) that flat 3.0 override
        // completely bypassed the velocity cap, forcing the slowest possible
        // bullet (bulletSpeed = 20 - 3*3 = 11) at exactly the moments (enemy
        // low on energy, or us far ahead) where we'd keep re-triggering this
        // override turn after turn. Real match trace (round 1 sim_149.jsonl vs
        // barriosnahuel__tirolio) showed us getting stuck in this state for
        // 600+ consecutive ticks once the enemy's energy first dropped <= 16:
        // we kept firing power-3.0 shots on a ~16-20 tick cycle, missing nearly
        // every one against their up-to-8-velocity erratic movement, bleeding
        // our own energy via bulletPower cost until we hit 0 and died (to a
        // wall hit, but only because we'd already spent nearly everything on
        // whiffed finishing shots) -- while the opponent's energy never dropped
        // further because we simply couldn't land hits at that bullet speed.
        // Fix: cap the forced "finishing"/"press advantage" power the same way
        // bulletPowerForDistance() already caps the normal distance-based power,
        // so a fast enemy still gets faster (more hittable) bullets even while
        // we're trying to close out the kill or press an energy lead, instead of
        // unconditionally reverting to the slowest bullet in the game.
        // Round 30: default (slow-enemy) cap lowered 3.0 -> 2.9, see the
        // matching comment in bulletPowerForDistance() above for the
        // analyze_power_accuracy.py evidence behind this change.
        // Round 109: keep this override's cap consistent with
        // bulletPowerForDistance()'s new 0.5 fast-mover cap (see that function's
        // comment for the full reasoning) -- otherwise the finishing/press-
        // advantage overrides would silently re-introduce the exact
        // below-breakeven 1.3-power bullet this round is trying to move away from
        // whenever the enemy is low on energy or we're ahead, which is precisely
        // when this override actually fires.
        double maxUsablePower = 2.9;
        double absEnemyVelocityForCap = Math.abs(e.getVelocity());
        if (absEnemyVelocityForCap > 6.0) {
            maxUsablePower = 0.5;
        } else if (absEnemyVelocityForCap > 3.0) {
            maxUsablePower = 1.9;
        }
        if (e.getEnergy() <= 16) {
            bulletPower = maxUsablePower;
        } else if (getEnergy() - e.getEnergy() > 15) {
            bulletPower = maxUsablePower;
        }
        double bulletSpeed = 20 - 3 * bulletPower;

        // Predict enemy future position via tick-by-tick simulation, accounting for
        // both current velocity/heading AND the enemy's estimated turn rate
        // (enemyHeadingRate, radians/tick) -- i.e. approximate circular targeting.
        // This degrades gracefully to plain linear prediction when the enemy is
        // going straight (enemyHeadingRate ~= 0), but tracks curving/orbiting
        // enemies (e.g. bots doing their own perpendicular strafing, same as ours)
        // much better than assuming a fixed heading the whole way.
        double enemyHeading = e.getHeadingRadians();
        double enemyVelocity = e.getVelocity();

        // Estimate the enemy's turn rate from the change in heading since our last
        // scan of them. Guard against huge/garbage deltas across a scan gap (e.g.
        // after briefly losing radar lock) by only trusting recent, single/short-gap
        // scans; otherwise assume 0 (straight-line fallback).
        //
        // Round 9 tuning: against a genuinely erratic/zigzag opponent
        // (trex22__deepthought -- see README_agent.md round 9 notes), a *raw*,
        // single-tick heading-delta estimate is noisy: a bot that mostly goes
        // straight but occasionally makes a sharp one-tick correction would
        // otherwise cause us to extrapolate that single-tick turn as if it were a
        // sustained curve for many future ticks, aiming well off to the side of
        // where the target actually ends up once it stops turning. Smooth the
        // estimate with a simple low-pass filter (blend with the previous
        // estimate) so transient single-tick turns get damped rather than fully
        // trusted, while a genuinely sustained turn (many consecutive ticks with
        // the same sign) still converges to being tracked within a few scans.
        // Also clamp the raw per-tick estimate to a sane maximum magnitude
        // (a real robot's max turn rate tops out around 0.116 rad/tick -- 10
        // deg/tick at v=0, less at speed -- so anything wildly beyond that is
        // almost certainly scan-gap noise, not real motion) before blending, so
        // one noisy sample can't dominate the smoothed estimate.
        long scanGap = (prevEnemyScanTime >= 0) ? (getTime() - prevEnemyScanTime) : -1;
        if (scanGap > 0 && scanGap <= 3) {
            double rawRate = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                    enemyHeading - prevEnemyHeading) / scanGap;
            double maxRate = 0.15;
            if (rawRate > maxRate) rawRate = maxRate;
            if (rawRate < -maxRate) rawRate = -maxRate;
            enemyHeadingRate = 0.5 * enemyHeadingRate + 0.5 * rawRate;
        } else {
            enemyHeadingRate = 0;
        }
        prevEnemyHeading = enemyHeading;
        prevEnemyScanTime = getTime();

        double myX = getX();
        double myY = getY();
        double enemyX = myX + Math.sin(absBearing) * e.getDistance();
        double enemyY = myY + Math.cos(absBearing) * e.getDistance();

        double predictedX = enemyX;
        double predictedY = enemyY;
        double simHeading = enemyHeading;
        double fieldW = getBattleFieldWidth();
        double fieldH = getBattleFieldHeight();
        // Simulate forward tick-by-tick until the bullet would have caught up to the
        // predicted position (bounded loop so we can't spin forever if something
        // goes numerically weird, e.g. bulletSpeed ~ 0).
        for (int i = 0; i < 60; i++) {
            double distSoFar = Point2D.distance(myX, myY, predictedX, predictedY);
            double bulletTimeSoFar = distSoFar / bulletSpeed;
            if (bulletTimeSoFar <= i) {
                break;
            }
            simHeading += enemyHeadingRate;
            predictedX += Math.sin(simHeading) * enemyVelocity;
            predictedY += Math.cos(simHeading) * enemyVelocity;
            // Keep the simulated enemy inside the field (a real enemy can't walk
            // through walls either), so we don't aim at an impossible point.
            predictedX = Math.min(Math.max(predictedX, 18), fieldW - 18);
            predictedY = Math.min(Math.max(predictedY, 18), fieldH - 18);
        }

        // Clamp predicted position to inside the battlefield so we don't aim off-field.
        predictedX = Math.min(Math.max(predictedX, 18), getBattleFieldWidth() - 18);
        predictedY = Math.min(Math.max(predictedY, 18), getBattleFieldHeight() - 18);

        double gunTargetAngle = Math.atan2(predictedX - myX, predictedY - myY);
        double gunTurn = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                gunTargetAngle - getGunHeadingRadians());
        setTurnGunRightRadians(gunTurn);

        // Round 10 tuning: previously fired whenever gunTurn < a flat 0.2 rad
        // (~11.4 deg) regardless of range. At long range that's a huge positional
        // slop -- e.g. at 550px, 0.2 rad of angular error is ~109px of miss
        // distance, several times a robot's ~18px half-width -- so plenty of
        // shots were being released while still clearly aimed off-target,
        // wasting energy and dragging accuracy down. Instead, size the firing
        // angle threshold to the target's actual angular half-width (atan(halfWidth
        // / distance)) plus a margin factor, so the tolerance shrinks
        // automatically at range (where precision matters most) while staying
        // generous up close (where the target's angular size is naturally large
        // and a tight threshold would just needlessly delay/skip easy shots).
        // Clamped to a sane [0.03, 0.22] range so we never demand impossible
        // precision nor keep the old overly-loose worst case.
        double targetHalfWidthAngle = Math.atan(20.0 / Math.max(e.getDistance(), 1));
        double fireThreshold = targetHalfWidthAngle * 1.3;
        if (fireThreshold < 0.03) fireThreshold = 0.03;
        if (fireThreshold > 0.22) fireThreshold = 0.22;

        if (getGunHeat() == 0 && Math.abs(gunTurn) < fireThreshold) {
            setFire(bulletPower);
        }

        // Round 20: if we're already in escape mode (started here or from
        // onHitRobot()), just keep reissuing the SAME escape command and skip all
        // other movement decisions below (ram-trigger, orbit) -- see the field
        // comments near escapeUntil/escapeHeadingRad for why this matters (event
        // priority ordering means onScannedRobot() and onHitRobot() would otherwise
        // silently overwrite each other's movement commands every tick).
        if (getTime() < escapeUntil) {
            reissueEscape();
            return;
        }

        // --- Stuck watchdog: if our velocity has been ~0 for several consecutive
        // scans (and we're not brand new / just started), we're probably wedged
        // against a wall or another robot. Force a strong escape burn toward the
        // field center instead of continuing normal orbit logic, which is exactly
        // the situation that got us stuck in the first place. ---
        if (Math.abs(getVelocity()) < 0.5) {
            stuckScanCount++;
        } else {
            stuckScanCount = 0;
        }

        if (stuckScanCount > 4) {
            double cx = getBattleFieldWidth() / 2.0;
            double cy = getBattleFieldHeight() / 2.0;
            double angleToCenter = Math.atan2(cx - myX, cy - myY);
            // Also flip strafe direction so once we break free we don't immediately
            // re-drive into the same wall.
            moveDirection = -moveDirection;
            strafeTimer = 0;
            beginEscape(angleToCenter, 60);
            return;
        }

        // --- Round 12 addition: opportunistic ramming. ---
        // Per Rules.html: ROBOT_HIT_DAMAGE = 0.6 energy taken by BOTH robots on any
        // robot-robot collision, plus ROBOT_HIT_BONUS = 1.2 *extra* damage dealt to
        // the target by whichever robot was moving forward into the collision. That
        // means initiating contact nets the initiator a relative energy swing of
        // +1.2 (they lose 0.6+1.2=1.8, we lose 0.6) -- a better swing-per-action than
        // almost any bullet at our current ~17% accuracy (see the bulletPower
        // comment above for the full swing(P,p) math), and it costs zero gun
        // energy/heat. Previously our movement logic only ever tried to hold
        // PREFERRED_DISTANCE or flee (onHitRobot always backed away), so we were
        // leaving this essentially free damage source completely unused, despite
        // round-0/1 logs showing plenty of long, grindy games decided by mutual
        // attrition -- exactly the situation where a free, bullet-independent
        // damage source should help convert ties into wins.
        // Trigger only when already very close (well inside our normal orbit
        // radius, so this never fights the wall-avoidance/stuck-watchdog logic
        // above, which always takes precedence via the early return) and we're not
        // critically low on energy ourselves (a collision still costs us 0.6, so
        // don't risk it if we're down to fumes).
        if (enemyDistance < 60 && getEnergy() > 3 && getTime() > rammingCooldownUntil) {
            double turnToEnemy = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                    absBearing - getHeadingRadians());
            setTurnRightRadians(turnToEnemy);
            setAhead(enemyDistance + 20);
            return;
        }

        // --- Movement: orbit / strafe around the enemy at preferred distance ---
        strafeTimer++;
        if (strafeTimer > 40 + (int) (Math.random() * 30)) {
            strafeTimer = 0;
            if (Math.random() < 0.4) {
                moveDirection = -moveDirection;
            }
        }

        // Round 44 addition ("corner camper" fix v2): round 43's "prefer the open
        // perpendicular side" heuristic only helps when an opponent is near a
        // SINGLE wall (half the orbit circle blocked) -- against an opponent
        // parked in an actual CORNER (up to 3/4 of the circle blocked), BOTH
        // perpendicular sides at the full PREFERRED_DISTANCE can be off-field, so
        // round 43's fix falls through to the reactive clamp-to-boundary fallback
        // just as often as before. Round 43's real-match result (see
        // README_agent.md) showed accuracy actually dropping (39% -> 31%) and
        // losses rising slightly (6 -> 8) against this exact corner-camping
        // opponent, with our own robot still wandering the ENTIRE battlefield
        // (x 18-782, y 18-582) rather than settling near the enemy -- consistent
        // with the "both sides blocked" case still happening often. Fix the root
        // geometric cause instead of reacting to it after the fact: shrink our
        // effective orbit radius toward however much clear space the enemy
        // actually has available near its current (possibly corner-hugging)
        // position, so a full or near-full orbit circle actually fits on-field to
        // begin with. Compute the enemy's approximate absolute position and its
        // clearance to the nearest wall on each axis; clamp our preferred distance
        // down to fit within that clearance (with a floor so we never crowd all
        // the way into ramming range purely from this effect).
        double enemyAbsX = myX + Math.sin(absBearing) * e.getDistance();
        double enemyAbsY = myY + Math.cos(absBearing) * e.getDistance();
        double enemyWallClearance = Math.min(
                Math.min(enemyAbsX, getBattleFieldWidth() - enemyAbsX),
                Math.min(enemyAbsY, getBattleFieldHeight() - enemyAbsY));
        double effectivePreferredDistance = Math.max(90,
                Math.min(PREFERRED_DISTANCE, enemyWallClearance - 20));

        double distanceError = e.getDistance() - effectivePreferredDistance;
        // Move perpendicular to enemy bearing (strafe), plus adjust toward/away
        // from enemy to hold preferred distance.
        double perpendicularAngle = absBearing + (Math.PI / 2) * moveDirection;

        double moveAmount = 100;
        if (Math.abs(distanceError) > 50) {
            // Bias speed slightly to correct distance while still strafing.
            moveAmount = (distanceError > 0) ? 120 : 80;
        }

        // Round 47 addition: real radial distance-closing component.
        // Traced this rung's 34 losses (33 losses + 1 draw, see README_agent.md)
        // against a corner-camping opponent and found our own robot's distance to
        // the enemy oscillating between ~270-680px for an ENTIRE game, never
        // settling anywhere near PREFERRED_DISTANCE (220) or even the shrunk
        // effectivePreferredDistance (as low as 90 near a corner, round 44) -- our
        // own position spanned nearly the whole battlefield (18-690 x, 42-454 y)
        // while the enemy stayed camped in a ~75x67 box the whole game. Root cause:
        // this movement code has NEVER had an actual radial (toward/away from
        // enemy) component -- moveAmount only changes the *magnitude* of a purely
        // PERPENDICULAR strafe move (still always tangential to the enemy), so a
        // large distance error (as when effectivePreferredDistance shrinks sharply
        // near a corner) was never being corrected by anything more than a +/-20
        // magnitude tweak on an orthogonal-to-the-fix direction -- hence the
        // circling-at-a-huge-radius, whole-battlefield-spanning pattern observed.
        // Fix: blend the perpendicular strafe direction with an explicit radial
        // direction (toward the enemy if too far, away if too close), with a
        // blend weight that grows with how large the distance error is (capped),
        // so small/normal-range errors barely change existing orbit behavior (low
        // risk of regressing already-healthy matchups) but large errors (as when
        // effectivePreferredDistance has shrunk a lot) actually pull us toward a
        // reachable orbit radius instead of just circling forever at the wrong one.
        double radialWeight = Math.min(0.75, Math.abs(distanceError) / 400.0);
        double radialAngle = (distanceError > 0) ? absBearing : (absBearing + Math.PI);
        double blendX = Math.sin(perpendicularAngle) * (1.0 - radialWeight)
                + Math.sin(radialAngle) * radialWeight;
        double blendY = Math.cos(perpendicularAngle) * (1.0 - radialWeight)
                + Math.cos(radialAngle) * radialWeight;
        if (Math.abs(blendX) > 1e-6 || Math.abs(blendY) > 1e-6) {
            perpendicularAngle = Math.atan2(blendX, blendY);
        }

        double minX = WALL_MARGIN;
        double maxX = getBattleFieldWidth() - WALL_MARGIN;
        double minY = WALL_MARGIN;
        double maxY = getBattleFieldHeight() - WALL_MARGIN;

        // Project the planned waypoint and clamp it to a safe inset rectangle so we
        // never issue a move that would drive us straight into (or through) a wall.
        // This is the core fix for the "wall standoff" bug: previously we always
        // moved along perpendicularAngle regardless of what was there, which could
        // repeatedly ram a wall and fight against onHitWall()'s recovery forever.
        double targetX = myX + Math.sin(perpendicularAngle) * moveAmount;
        double targetY = myY + Math.cos(perpendicularAngle) * moveAmount;

        boolean clamped = (targetX < minX || targetX > maxX || targetY < minY || targetY > maxY);

        // Round 43 addition ("corner camper" fix): if an opponent parks itself near
        // (or inside) the WALL_MARGIN inset -- e.g. a stationary/sentry-style bot
        // that just sits in a corner -- roughly HALF of the circle around it at
        // PREFERRED_DISTANCE is off-field, so whichever perpendicular side
        // moveDirection happens to be pointing at that moment is a coin flip
        // between "open space" and "immediately clamped". Analysis of this rung's
        // 6 losses (see README_agent.md round 43 notes) showed our own robot
        // wandering the ENTIRE battlefield (full 18-782 x-range, 18-582 y-range)
        // while the enemy stayed camped in one small corner the whole game, plus
        // elevated wall-hit counts (7-15/game vs a ~2.2/game series average) --
        // consistent with repeatedly picking the clamped side and reacting after
        // the fact, rather than settling into a stable orbit on the enemy's open
        // side. Fix: if the primary perpendicular choice is clamped, check whether
        // flipping to the OTHER perpendicular side is clear; if so, commit to that
        // side (flip moveDirection persistently, not just for this tick) instead of
        // falling through to the reactive clamp-to-boundary fallback below.
        if (clamped) {
            double altAngle = absBearing - (Math.PI / 2) * moveDirection;
            double altX = myX + Math.sin(altAngle) * moveAmount;
            double altY = myY + Math.cos(altAngle) * moveAmount;
            boolean altClamped = (altX < minX || altX > maxX || altY < minY || altY > maxY);
            if (!altClamped) {
                moveDirection = -moveDirection;
                perpendicularAngle = altAngle;
                targetX = altX;
                targetY = altY;
                clamped = false;
                strafeTimer = 0;
            }
        }

        double moveAngle;
        double finalMoveAmount;
        if (clamped) {
            // Actually clamp the waypoint into the safe rectangle now (this was
            // previously done inline while computing `clamped` above; kept as a
            // separate step now since round 43's "prefer open side" check above
            // needs the *unclamped* targetX/targetY to test the alternate side
            // fairly, before we commit to clamping as a last resort here).
            if (targetX < minX) targetX = minX;
            if (targetX > maxX) targetX = maxX;
            if (targetY < minY) targetY = minY;
            if (targetY > maxY) targetY = maxY;
            // Re-derive heading/distance toward the clamped (safe) waypoint instead
            // of blindly driving perpendicular into the wall.
            double dx = targetX - myX;
            double dy = targetY - myY;
            double distToWaypoint = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
            if (distToWaypoint < 10) {
                // We're already basically at the safe boundary; head toward the
                // field center instead so we don't just sit here.
                double cx = getBattleFieldWidth() / 2.0;
                double cy = getBattleFieldHeight() / 2.0;
                moveAngle = Math.atan2(cx - myX, cy - myY);
                finalMoveAmount = 100;
            } else {
                moveAngle = Math.atan2(dx, dy);
                finalMoveAmount = Math.min(distToWaypoint, moveAmount);
            }
        } else {
            moveAngle = perpendicularAngle;
            finalMoveAmount = moveAmount;
        }

        double turnToMove = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                moveAngle - getHeadingRadians());
        setTurnRightRadians(turnToMove);
        setAhead(finalMoveAmount);

    }

    private double bulletPowerForDistance(double distance, double enemyVelocity) {
        // Round 7 tuning pushed power up across all bands and it paid off: round 1's
        // real match (this same opponent, it_economics__ite_bomax, still barely moves
        // at avg speed 0.8 and only 4% accuracy against us) showed accuracy actually
        // rose slightly (69% -> 70%) and team score rose (38929 -> 39892) with zero
        // win-rate cost. Round 8 pushes the middle bands a bit further still (2.6->2.9,
        // 2.0->2.2) and the long-range band a touch (1.3->1.5), on the same reasoning:
        // this opponent doesn't meaningfully punish us for slower bullets, so more
        // damage-per-hit should keep raising total damage dealt/score. Close-range band
        // stays at 3.0, the Robocode engine's max bullet power. If a future, more
        // mobile/accurate opponent shows a win-rate or accuracy regression here, revert
        // toward archive/round1_backups/MyTank.java.before_round8_tuning (round 7 values:
        // 2.6/2.0/1.3) or even further back to .before_round7_tuning (original 2.2/1.5/1.0).
        // Round 30 tuning: rounds 28/29's analyze_power_accuracy.py findings (see
        // README_agent.md) showed the flat-3.0 close-range band consistently
        // performing WORSE, or no better, than the 150-350/2.9-power "sweet spot"
        // band across 2+ independent opponent samples (round 28: 2.9-band 2nd-best
        // of 5 buckets; round 29/this environment's round 1: 2.9-band BEST of 5
        // buckets at 63.9% vs the 3.0-heavy bucket's 36.0%) -- consistent with the
        // Rules.class-derived swing(P,p) math from round 12's notes once you plug in
        // the *real* observed per-bucket p values instead of a flat overall
        // accuracy: even accounting for 2.9 dealing very slightly less damage per
        // hit than 3.0, the accuracy gap more than makes up for it. Lowered the
        // close-range band from 3.0 -> 2.9 to match the already-tuned 150-350 band
        // (no longer a meaningfully special "max power" case) and applied the same
        // 2.9 cap to onScannedRobot()'s "finishing"/"press advantage" override
        // (previously flat 3.0 for a slow enemy) for consistency, since those
        // overrides are exactly what was polluting the old "3.0-3.5" bucket with a
        // large volume of below-average-accuracy shots. If a future round's
        // trace.md/analyze_power_accuracy.py shows this was wrong (e.g. accuracy or
        // score drops against a new opponent), revert via
        // archive/round1_backups/MyTank.java.before_round30_cap29.
        double power;
        if (distance < 150) {
            power = 2.9;
        } else if (distance < 350) {
            power = 2.9;
        } else if (distance < 550) {
            power = 2.2;
        } else {
            power = 1.5;
        }

        // Round 17 addition: the round 7/8 tuning above was validated against
        // near-stationary/slow opponents (it_economics__ite_bomax, avg speed 0.8;
        // kinnla__antiwalls, avg speed 1.0) where bullet SPEED barely matters
        // because the target isn't going anywhere fast regardless of how long our
        // bullet takes to arrive. This round's opponent (barriosnahuel__tirolio,
        // see README_agent.md round 17 notes) is a genuinely fast, erratic mover
        // (observed velocity up to ~8, the game's max) that almost never fires
        // back itself -- match logs showed us losing 2/250 games purely by
        // bleeding our OWN energy to 0 via a long string of missed high-power
        // shots (each costing bulletPower energy up front, hit or miss) while the
        // enemy's energy stayed flat because it wasn't shooting either. Since
        // bulletSpeed = 20 - 3*power, HIGHER power means a SLOWER bullet, which
        // gives a fast/erratic target strictly more time-of-flight to change
        // direction before the bullet arrives -- i.e. against a fast mover,
        // higher power doesn't just cost more per shot, it also directly lowers
        // hit probability (unlike against a slow/stationary target, where p is
        // roughly power-independent, which is what round 7/8/12's swing(P,p) math
        // implicitly assumed). Throttle power down when the enemy is moving fast,
        // trading some damage-per-hit for meaningfully faster bullets (better
        // real hit chance) and cheaper misses, without touching the bands above
        // at all for slow/stationary opponents (absVelocity ~= 0 keeps the exact
        // round 7/8 behavior).
        // Round 109 addition: rounds 107/108 found that kcanida__pikachu (by far
        // the toughest opponent in this file's history alongside pez__gf1, rounds
        // 11-12/107-108) exposed our own round-17 fast-mover cap (1.3) as running
        // BELOW its own breakeven accuracy: analyze_power_accuracy.py on round
        // 108's revert baseline showed our absVelocity>6 bucket (power 1.3) at
        // only 9.7% accuracy, while round-12's swing(P,p)=p*(9P-2)-P breakeven at
        // P=1.3 is ~13.4% -- i.e. we were LOSING energy on average every time we
        // fired in this bucket. Round 107 tried fixing this by tightening the
        // fire-angle threshold (fire less often, hoping for higher-quality shots)
        // -- this BACKFIRED badly in a real match (accuracy dropped further to
        // 5.5% in that bucket, win rate 37%->24%, see round 108's trace) and was
        // reverted. This round tries a different, mathematically safer lever
        // instead: for bulletPower <= 1, Rules.getBulletDamage() is a flat 4*P
        // (not 6*P-2) and getBulletHitBonus() is 3*P, so swing = P*(7p-1) -- the
        // breakeven accuracy (p=1/7=14.3%) is CONSTANT regardless of P in (0,1].
        // This means dropping power well below 1.3 costs us nothing in terms of
        // "how accurate do we need to be to break even" while making the bullet
        // meaningfully faster (bulletSpeed=20-3*P: 16.1 at P=1.3 vs 18.5 at
        // P=0.5), which should make it more likely to actually land against a
        // fast/erratic mover -- exactly mirroring kcanida__pikachu's OWN observed
        // strategy (it fires the bulk of its own shots at very low power, 0-0.5,
        // and lands 16.1% accuracy there, well above our 9.7% at 1.3 power,
        // against the same matchup). Lowered the >6.0 cap from 1.3 to 0.5; left
        // the >3.0 cap (1.9) untouched since that bucket is NOT currently running
        // below breakeven (round 108's data: 1.5-2.0 bucket at 23.3% accuracy,
        // comfortably above its own ~14.9% breakeven at P=1.9) -- isolating this
        // one, mathematically-justified change so it's cleanly attributable if it
        // doesn't help. NOT yet validated by a real match (see README_agent.md
        // round 109 notes) -- given rounds 107/108's hard lesson that
        // theoretically-sound-looking fixes against this specific opponent can
        // still backfire, watch this bucket's real accuracy VERY closely next
        // round and revert via
        // archive/round1_backups/MyTank.java.before_round109_lowpower_fastcap if
        // it doesn't clearly improve (or if it gets worse).
        double absVelocity = Math.abs(enemyVelocity);
        if (absVelocity > 6.0) {
            power = Math.min(power, 0.5);
        } else if (absVelocity > 3.0) {
            power = Math.min(power, 1.9);
        }
        return power;
    }

    public void onHitByBullet(HitByBulletEvent e) {
        // Round 24: if we're already in escape mode (see escapeUntil/beginEscape
        // comments above), don't let this handler's own juke override the shared
        // escape command -- HitByBulletEvent's priority sits between HitRobotEvent
        // and ScannedRobotEvent, so without this check it could silently clobber
        // the escape command every tick we're stuck-ramming AND getting shot at,
        // exactly the same "handler overrides handler" bug class round 20 fixed
        // for onHitRobot/onScannedRobot but which onHitWall/onHitByBullet were
        // never updated to respect.
        if (getTime() < escapeUntil) {
            reissueEscape();
            return;
        }
        // Reverse strafing direction and juke.
        moveDirection = -moveDirection;
        strafeTimer = 0;
        setTurnRightRadians(robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                Math.PI / 2 - e.getBearingRadians()));
        setAhead(60 * moveDirection);
    }

    public void onHitWall(HitWallEvent e) {
        // Round 24: same escape-mode-priority fix as onHitByBullet above.
        // HitWallEvent's priority (30) sits between HitRobotEvent (40) and
        // ScannedRobotEvent (10) -- if we're stuck-ramming in a corner (a very
        // common way to trigger a WALL hit on the SAME tick, since the corner
        // itself is what pinned us), this handler firing with its own
        // steer-toward-center command was silently overriding the escape
        // mechanism's rotating-heading logic every such tick, which is exactly
        // why round 23's rotating-escape fix didn't actually reduce STUCK-RAMMING
        // freeze durations in real matches (see README_agent.md round 24 notes).
        if (getTime() < escapeUntil) {
            reissueEscape();
            return;
        }
        // Round 34: found (via real-match log traces, see README_agent.md round
        // 34 notes) that onHitWall() itself was the *source* of repeated,
        // multi-tick "wedged at the wall" freezes (position frozen at an edge
        // coordinate, v=0.0, HIT_WALL re-firing every tick, for anywhere from a
        // couple to a dozen+ consecutive ticks, several separate times per game)
        // in real losing/tying games -- NOT just a victim of being overridden by
        // onScannedRobot() (that priority-ordering risk was already handled by
        // the escapeUntil check above). The bug: this handler used to issue its
        // own one-shot turn-toward-center + setAhead() command WITHOUT ever
        // calling beginEscape()/beginNoTurnEscape() to enter the shared escape
        // mode -- so it never told onScannedRobot() "I'm handling this", and on
        // the very next tick onScannedRobot() (or this handler again, with a
        // freshly-recomputed and possibly still-blocked turn) would silently
        // clobber/repeat the command with no stuck-detection at all, exactly the
        // same "handler doesn't coordinate" bug class rounds 20/25 already fixed
        // for onHitRobot(). Round 34's fix entered the no-turn escape mode here.
        //
        // Round 35: found (via real-match log traces against a NEW opponent,
        // andrekorol__oppswantmedead, see README_agent.md round 35 notes) that
        // round 34's no-turn choice was itself wrong for the wall-only case:
        // traced sim_54.jsonl and found our robot bouncing back and forth
        // between TWO DIFFERENT walls of the same corner for 200+ ticks (e.g.
        // oscillating between (768.7,18.0) near the top wall and (782.0,45.7)
        // near the right wall), losing ~3 energy per bounce, purely because
        // the no-turn escape's ahead/back alternation is restricted to a single
        // fixed axis (whatever heading we happened to be facing when we hit the
        // wall) -- in an actual corner, NEITHER "ahead" nor "back" along that
        // one axis points into the open; only a diagonal turn does. Unlike
        // HitRobotEvent, HitWallEvent's javadoc documents no isMyFault-style
        // turn-cancellation mechanic (round 25's actual root-cause finding was
        // specific to *robot* contact) -- so there's no known reason turning
        // should be unsafe here, and round 23's rotating-heading turn-based
        // escape (which naturally searches all 4 quadrants and defaults to
        // heading toward the field center, i.e. away from any single corner)
        // is a much better fit for a wall-only stuck condition than the
        // single-axis no-turn mode. Switched back to the turn-based
        // beginEscape(), aimed at the field center, for this handler
        // specifically -- onHitRobot()'s ramming-lock case (where the
        // isMyFault mechanism WAS directly confirmed via a frozen-heading
        // trace, round 25) still correctly uses the no-turn variant, unchanged.
        moveDirection = -moveDirection;
        strafeTimer = 0;
        double cx = getBattleFieldWidth() / 2.0;
        double cy = getBattleFieldHeight() / 2.0;
        double angleToCenter = Math.atan2(cx - getX(), cy - getY());
        beginEscape(angleToCenter, 60);
    }

    public void onHitRobot(HitRobotEvent e) {
        // Safety net: keep the radar spinning even if for some reason the main
        // run() loop's own re-arm check (see the CRITICAL RADAR-STALL FIX comment
        // above) doesn't get a chance to run this turn -- cheap insurance against
        // the round-14 stuck-ramming bug (see below) also freezing the radar.
        if (getRadarTurnRemaining() == 0) {
            setTurnRadarRight(Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY);
        }

        // Round 20: if we're already in escape mode, just keep reissuing the same
        // escape command and skip the press-forward/disengage decision below
        // entirely -- see the escapeUntil/escapeHeadingRad field comments for why
        // (this handler and onScannedRobot() must never independently decide
        // different movement commands in the same tick, or whichever one runs
        // last -- always onScannedRobot(), since HitRobotEvent's default priority
        // 40 is processed before ScannedRobotEvent's default priority 10 -- will
        // silently discard the other's decision, which is exactly the bug this
        // round's fix targets).
        if (getTime() < escapeUntil) {
            reissueEscape();
            return;
        }

        // Round 14 bugfix: detect a "stuck ramming" mutual-collision lock before
        // deciding whether to press forward. Real match logs (see README_agent.md
        // round 14 notes) showed games where round 12's "always press forward
        // into the enemy when healthy" logic below gets stuck: HitRobotEvent kept
        // firing every tick for 100+ consecutive ticks with our x/y AND radar/gun
        // heading completely frozen (velocity pinned at 0), bleeding 0.6
        // energy/tick in pure collision damage (confirmed via per-tick log replay,
        // not inactivity decay) with zero benefit -- one observed case lost ~86
        // energy this way in a single game we still happened to win only because
        // the opponent was even weaker. This looks like it happens when the enemy
        // is itself wedged (e.g. against a wall) and our repeated "charge toward
        // enemy" command can't make real progress, but the collision keeps
        // re-triggering forever with nothing to break the loop.
        //
        // Track whether our position has actually changed since the last
        // HitRobotEvent. If it hasn't (within a few px) for 2+ consecutive
        // collisions, we're stuck -- stop charging and forcibly disengage
        // (turn away from the enemy and back off) instead of re-charging into
        // the same wall/robot corner again.
        double myX = getX();
        double myY = getY();
        boolean stationarySinceLastHit = !Double.isNaN(lastHitRobotX)
                && Math.hypot(myX - lastHitRobotX, myY - lastHitRobotY) < 3;
        if (stationarySinceLastHit) {
            hitRobotStationaryCount++;
        } else {
            hitRobotStationaryCount = 0;
        }
        lastHitRobotX = myX;
        lastHitRobotY = myY;

        // Round 25: lowered from >=2 to >=1. Per the isMyFault mechanism
        // documented above, once we're in continued contact, re-pressing
        // "forward" every tick gives no further ROBOT_HIT_BONUS (that's only
        // awarded on the tick a fresh moving-into collision actually happens,
        // per observed real-match energy traces showing only the flat 0.6
        // ROBOT_HIT_DAMAGE per tick during a static lock, not the extra 1.2)
        // while *also* risking freezing our own turning via isMyFault -- so
        // there's no reason to wait for a second confirmation once we've
        // already seen one stationary hit; disengage immediately.
        boolean stuckRamming = hitRobotStationaryCount >= 1;

        // Round 12 change (still applies for the normal case): collision handling
        // depends on our health, to work with (not against) the opportunistic-
        // ramming logic in onScannedRobot(). Contact damage already happened the
        // instant this event fired regardless of what we do next -- backing away
        // doesn't undo it. Since initiating/continuing contact is a favorable
        // relative-energy trade (+1.2 swing per exchange, no gun energy cost) as
        // long as we're not critically low AND we're actually making progress
        // (not stuck -- see above), keep pressing forward when healthy.
        if (getEnergy() > 8 && !stuckRamming) {
            double turnToEnemy = robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(
                    e.getBearingRadians());
            setTurnRightRadians(turnToEnemy);
            setAhead(40);
            return;
        }

        // Disengage: either we're critically low on energy (old behavior), or
        // we've detected a stuck-ramming lock (new this round). Turn away from
        // the enemy (opposite of its bearing) and back off, breaking the mutual-
        // collision loop, and put the opportunistic-ramming trigger in
        // onScannedRobot() on a brief cooldown so we don't immediately re-charge
        // straight back into the same stuck situation.
        //
        // ROUND 19 BUGFIX (see README_agent.md round 19 notes): this used to turn
        // to face directly AWAY from the enemy and then call setBack(80). But
        // setBack() moves *opposite* the robot's current heading -- so after
        // turning to face away from the enemy, setBack(80) actually drove us
        // BACK TOWARD the enemy, the exact opposite of the intended disengage!
        // Real match log analysis (round 19, sim_0.jsonl from a match vs
        // pez__droidpoet) found a game where our tank got wedged in a corner
        // (pinned against the left wall AND vertically adjacent to the enemy,
        // itself also wall-stuck) for 178 consecutive ticks with this exact
        // "disengage" branch firing every tick and never making any progress --
        // we only escaped because the enemy happened to die first, not because
        // the disengage logic ever worked. Also blend in a pull toward the
        // field center (not just directly away from the enemy) so we don't
        // just drive further along/into whatever wall we're already pinned
        // against -- directly analogous to onHitWall()'s existing center-facing
        // fix, applied here for the first time to the ramming-disengage path.
        // NOTE: e.getBearingRadians() is relative to OUR current heading, not an
        // absolute field angle -- must convert to an absolute bearing before
        // mixing it with angleToCenter (which IS already an absolute field
        // angle, via atan2 on field coordinates) or the vector blend below
        // would be mixing two different reference frames and produce a
        // meaningless result.
        // Round 25 REPLACEMENT of the round-19/20 turn-based disengage: real
        // match traces (see README_agent.md round 25 notes, sim_7.jsonl vs
        // it_economics__ite_ctbot) showed this turn-based approach (compute an
        // "away + toward center" absolute heading, then beginEscape() to turn
        // toward it) still froze body heading AND velocity at exactly 0 for
        // 60+ consecutive ticks in real games, because of the isMyFault
        // mechanism documented above the escapeNoTurn field: while our
        // commanded motion still counts as "toward" the enemy (true for
        // every partial-turn tick before the turn completes), the engine
        // cancels our rotation too, so we could never actually complete the
        // turn toward the "safe" heading in the first place -- a total
        // deadlock that only ever resolved when the enemy died first.
        //
        // Fix: don't turn at all. e.getBearingRadians() tells us whether the
        // enemy is roughly ahead of our CURRENT heading (|bearing| < 90deg)
        // or roughly behind it. Either way, moving along our EXISTING heading
        // in the direction away from the enemy (setBack() if they're ahead,
        // setAhead() if they're behind) requires no rotation at all, so it
        // can never trigger the isMyFault-blocks-turning trap -- it either
        // works immediately (if that direction is physically clear) or, if
        // still blocked after a few ticks (e.g. a wall is back there
        // instead), reissueEscape()'s no-turn stuck-detection flips to the
        // opposite direction and tries that instead (see reissueEscape()).
        boolean enemyAhead = Math.abs(
                robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(e.getBearingRadians()))
                < Math.PI / 2.0;
        moveDirection = -moveDirection;
        strafeTimer = 0;
        hitRobotStationaryCount = 0;
        rammingCooldownUntil = getTime() + 30;
        beginNoTurnEscape(enemyAhead, 30);
    }
}
